\title{Small-Field Commitments and Ring Switching}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    Committing to values that live in a tiny field without paying extension-field prices for every one of them. The problem [Binius](dhsorens-003J) exists to solve, and the reason ArkLib has a \code{RingSwitching} directory.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{Why a naive commitment to #{\mathbb{F}_2} values wastes almost all of its capacity, and what packing recovers.}
        \li{The ring-switching reduction: a claim over the extension becomes a claim over the base, or the reverse.}
        \li{Where soundness needs the large field regardless, and how much of the argument can stay small.}
        \li{The interaction with [binary towers](dhsorens-002L) specifically, versus 31-bit primes.}
        \li{What ArkLib formalises here and what remains open.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [ArkLib](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/ArkLib), [The Small-Field Turn](https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1371).
}
